WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:04.420
Thanks to Audible — the world’s leading
provider of audiobooks — for sponsoring

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:06.630
this video.

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:12.080
Deep in Eastern China’s Anhui province,
situated in a valley between the mountains,

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.650
is a small town called Maotanchang.

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:20.779
Of the hundreds of cities and thousands of
towns and villages in the world’s most populous

00:00:20.779 --> 00:00:27.199
country, Maotanchang is, without a doubt,
the most boring of them all.

00:00:27.199 --> 00:00:32.649
Unlike even the drabbest of industrial mining
or factory towns, here there’s nothing even

00:00:32.649 --> 00:00:34.989
resembling entertainment.

00:00:34.989 --> 00:00:40.629
No bowling alleys, theaters, water parks,
bars, or internet cafes.

00:00:40.629 --> 00:00:47.270
A game shop once opened, but was promptly
forced to close after locals led a boycott.

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:53.449
Its busiest places: a post office, grocery
store, and a few scattered restaurants, close

00:00:53.449 --> 00:00:58.949
early and stay quiet enough that they could
only be doing so intentionally.

00:00:58.949 --> 00:01:06.160
But make no mistake: Maotanchang is not a
forgotten pit-stop, slowly fading-away to

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:07.160
history.

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:09.750
Actually, just the opposite.

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:15.450
Thousands flock to this 4,000-person town,
hours away from any major city — one with

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:18.970
no historical or religious significance.

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:24.840
Though there are no jobs to be had, there
is a factory, some would argue, though not

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.140
the kind you’d expect.

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:33.050
Its private high school is known, across China,
as a so-called “Study factory” where parents

00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:38.119
bring their children to prepare for the most
important exam of their lives.

00:01:38.119 --> 00:01:44.590
Its remote location and lifeless streets are
deliberate features — there’s simply nothing

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:46.850
to do but study.

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:52.880
As the town has grown in prominence, its population
has doubled — from 4 to 8,000 — the new

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:59.039
residents being the families of test-takers,
who, in turn, drive real estate prices sky-high

00:01:59.039 --> 00:02:03.250
and consume roughly 90% of the local economy.

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:09.940
The test is called the Gaokao — ‘Gao’
meaning high, as-in higher education, i.e.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:13.190
university, and ‘Kao’ meaning exam.

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:18.120
These days, around 10 million 3rd-year high
school students — graduating seniors in

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:23.080
the Chinese education system — take the
test each year, making it a candidate for

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:25.660
the largest anywhere on earth.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:32.890
In normal years, the 9-hour exam begins on
June 7th and lasts 2 or 3 days, depending

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:34.640
on the province.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:39.460
Although the exam is coordinated and synced
across the whole of China, excluding the special

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:44.810
administrative regions of Hong Kong and Macau,
the questions and logistics are arranged by

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:48.010
each province and provincial-level city.

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:53.950
For most students it consists of math, Chinese,
and a foreign language — usually English,

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:59.530
but alternatively Japanese, Russian, or French
— plus his or her choice of either humanities

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:05.360
— which includes geography, history, and
politics, or science — meaning biology,

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:07.420
chemistry, and physics.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:12.230
The questions are mainly multiple choice and
short answers, though the Chinese section

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:16.370
includes an essay, scored by two random graders.

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:21.040
Before or afterward, students list the schools
they wish to attend.

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:26.540
By the time scores are released in July, provincial
authorities have negotiated their quota for

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:31.620
admission to each university, which determines
its lowest accepted score.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:39.740
The maximum number of points is 750, though
anything above even 700 is considered legendary,

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:43.610
thanks to its notoriously inscrutable questions.

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:48.890
When all is said and done, around 10% of students
receive offers to one of the “Tier 1”,

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:54.250
best schools in the country, and 20% end up
in a 2nd Tier school.

00:03:54.250 --> 00:04:00.000
The highest scorers in each province become
overnight celebrities — their photos plastered

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.930
throughout their hometowns, parents praised
on television, and their choice of school

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:07.740
subject to much scrutiny.

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:12.790
From a distance, the Gaokao may seem like
a larger and slightly more elaborate version

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:17.620
of the SAT, ACT, A-Levels, or JEE.

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:20.370
But such an image would be wildly mistaken.

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:25.610
For starters, the Gaokao is only offered once
per year.

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:29.890
Meaning that if a student isn’t accepted
into one of his or her selected schools, of

00:04:29.890 --> 00:04:34.460
which there can only be a few, they will have
no choice but to wait until the following

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:38.790
June, which many do, sometimes several times
over.

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:44.000
To be precise, only about 8 of the 10 million
test-takers are accepted.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:52.160
The other roughly 20% are faced with a very
unenviable decision — wait another 365 days,

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:54.229
or give up.

00:04:54.229 --> 00:04:58.860
Parents often resent those students who choose
to take the exam again — saying they drive

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:04.400
up the average score at the expense of their
child, sometimes despite already being accepted

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:06.560
to one or more schools.

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:12.770
Gaokao scores are also, in most cases, the
only criteria for college admission.

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:18.690
The concept of recommendations, personal essays,
and extracurriculars are so foreign to Chinese

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:23.520
families that they usually hire a counselor
to guide them through this process when applying

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:26.039
to a overseas university.

00:05:26.039 --> 00:05:32.940
The Gaokao is very much make or break, which
is why it has spawned such a lucrative industry,

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:37.219
of which Maotanchang is one of the biggest
benefactors.

00:05:37.219 --> 00:05:43.199
What sets it apart from all the other schools,
tutoring centers, and methods is that, quite

00:05:43.199 --> 00:05:45.789
simply, it really works.

00:05:45.789 --> 00:05:53.020
It claims a 90% university admissions rate,
compared to the 30% of its province as a whole.

00:05:53.020 --> 00:05:56.219
Of course, this success comes at a price.

00:05:56.219 --> 00:05:58.340
And quite a steep one.

00:05:58.340 --> 00:06:04.240
The campus is run more like a military training
academy than a school — with 16-hours of

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:06.910
studying scheduled per day.

00:06:06.910 --> 00:06:10.490
Everything is designed and optimized for studying.

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:17.590
For example, there are no laundry rooms, electrical
outlets, or, for many years, even hot water.

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:24.610
Visitors, including parents, are allowed on
campus for exactly 3 hours each Sunday afternoon.

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:30.539
The security guards, who drive around on golf
carts, ensure these rules are followed.

00:06:30.539 --> 00:06:36.789
Its real secret formula, however, is the way
it meticulously ranks, rewards, and punishes

00:06:36.789 --> 00:06:41.139
its 500 teachers, alongside their students.

00:06:41.139 --> 00:06:46.210
Their salaries are already higher than average,
but what drives them to essentially give up

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:51.289
on their lives — quit their jobs, hold off
on starting a family, and move to the middle

00:06:51.289 --> 00:06:53.949
of nowhere — are the bonuses.

00:06:53.949 --> 00:07:00.380
If a student is accepted to China’s famous
Peking or Tsinghua Universities, say, then

00:07:00.380 --> 00:07:07.569
50,000 RMB, or about $8,000 US Dollars, is
divided between all of his or her teachers.

00:07:07.569 --> 00:07:13.529
A good educator, therefore, can easily double
or triple his or her salary.

00:07:13.529 --> 00:07:20.112
On the flip side, the lowest-ranked teachers
each year can expect to be fired.

00:07:20.112 --> 00:07:24.960
To many people, these descriptions elicit
pity, if not outrage.

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:30.639
Whenever an image or anecdote of a 6-foot
pile of books or the school’s 16-hour schedule

00:07:30.639 --> 00:07:38.259
goes viral, a certain fury follows — “How
dare someone try to pay to game a test?”

00:07:38.259 --> 00:07:42.099
or “What an incredible waste of young people’s
lives”.

00:07:42.099 --> 00:07:48.059
In reality, the vast majority of Maotanchang
students are not rich kids from the city but,

00:07:48.059 --> 00:07:53.199
rather, children of poor rural families who
have saved for years.

00:07:53.199 --> 00:07:59.760
For them, the Gaokao is a route — their
only route — to upward social mobility,

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:03.259
and Maotanchang is their golden ticket.

00:08:03.259 --> 00:08:09.229
Which is why, when the day finally comes,
it’s one of hope and celebration, not dread

00:08:09.229 --> 00:08:10.740
or hatred.

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:15.729
To an observer, June 7th may look like the
beginning of a festival.

00:08:15.729 --> 00:08:18.789
Parents book hotel rooms near the testing
venues.

00:08:18.789 --> 00:08:24.809
Rows of busses full of students roam the cities,
escorted by a parade of their families.

00:08:24.809 --> 00:08:29.600
Cars are forbidden from honking their horns,
construction sites within half a mile are

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:35.560
ordered to stay silent, and over 1 million
supervisors across the country proctor its

00:08:35.560 --> 00:08:37.620
7,000 exam sites.

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:43.910
Then, on the final day, after their final
stroke is written, students overwhelmingly

00:08:43.910 --> 00:08:48.480
report the same unexpected feeling: loss.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:53.840
While these 9 fateful hours have consumed
nearly every second and thought of the last

00:08:53.840 --> 00:09:01.660
few years of their lives, if not entire education,
the Gaokao also acts as a powerful goal, a

00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:07.500
singular milestone far in the future and yet
close enough to always instill urgency.

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:12.250
The Gaokao is not a thing to be romanticized.

00:09:12.250 --> 00:09:18.140
Critics rightly emphasize the unfathomable
mental strain it places on students — strain

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:24.830
which sometimes spirals into permanent damage,
the years of lives it robs of every boy and

00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:32.190
girl, the unnecessary rote memorization which
is never recalled, and the permanent destiny

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:35.510
it prescribes to those deemed unfit.

00:09:35.510 --> 00:09:42.230
But such an all-encompassing task also provides
a concrete purpose that drives students forward

00:09:42.230 --> 00:09:47.640
and a universally-accepted excuse to all failures
non-academic.

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:53.600
One can be bad at just about everything, and
still be celebrated for scoring highly.

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:59.100
In some ways, the Gaokao is a mask — hiding
all accomplishments and challenges behind

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:02.410
a single, person-defining number.

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:05.720
And not only at the level of the individual.

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:10.980
Social and economic inequalities are also
rationalized and upheld by the meritocratic

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:13.100
nature of the Gaokao.

00:10:13.100 --> 00:10:18.250
It suggests that everyone gets what he or
she deserves, providing an excuse for huge

00:10:18.250 --> 00:10:20.560
differences in opportunity.

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:25.730
And while there have been many moves to reform
the Gaokao and even discussions about replacing

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:31.430
it entirely, the great irony is that those
who suffer the most, families and students

00:10:31.430 --> 00:10:36.280
themselves, are also sometimes its most vocal
supporters.

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:42.990
To them, the Gaokao may be the hardest test
in China, but, it’s also their only ticket

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:45.170
to a better life.

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:50.380
One of the inspirations for this video was
a book I read called “The Case Against Education”

00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:51.980
by Bryan Caplan.

00:10:51.980 --> 00:10:57.270
It explains what so many of us think but don’t
have the eloquent words to prove: how the

00:10:57.270 --> 00:11:00.460
modern education system fails us.

